# Cross-Repository Accessibility Mapping: Production Management and Audio Description

## Purpose

This document maps the production-management framework in [Accessible Film, TV and Video Content Production Management](https://github.com/Double-FW/Accessible-Film-TV-and-Video-Content-Production-Management) to the editorial and production practices in [Audio Descriptions](https://github.com/Double-FW/Audio-Descriptions).

It shows how to manage audio description (AD) as a versioned, resourced access workstream: choose an appropriate access route, brief the people doing the work, review it against the actual cut, and retain evidence for delivery and release decisions.

This is a crosswalk, not a replacement for either repository’s guidance, a legal specification, or a claim that every programme requires a separate AD track.

## Source documents

- Production-management framework: [`AGENTS.md`](https://github.com/Double-FW/Accessible-Film-TV-and-Video-Content-Production-Management/blob/main/AGENTS.md) and [Accessible TV Content: Guidance for Production Teams](https://github.com/Double-FW/Accessible-Film-TV-and-Video-Content-Production-Management/blob/main/Accessible_TV_Content_Guidance_for_Production_Teams.md).
- Audio Description guidance: [`README.md`](https://github.com/Double-FW/Audio-Descriptions/blob/main/README.md), [`AGENTS.md`](https://github.com/Double-FW/Audio-Descriptions/blob/main/AGENTS.md), and the [access-route decision](https://github.com/Double-FW/Audio-Descriptions/blob/main/docs/01-access-route.md), [production workflow](https://github.com/Double-FW/Audio-Descriptions/blob/main/docs/02-production-workflow.md), [editorial principles](https://github.com/Double-FW/Audio-Descriptions/blob/main/docs/04-ad-editorial-principles.md), [recording and delivery](https://github.com/Double-FW/Audio-Descriptions/blob/main/docs/06-recording-and-delivery.md), and [evaluation and QA](https://github.com/Double-FW/Audio-Descriptions/blob/main/docs/07-evaluation-and-qa.md).

The production-management framework supplies the lifecycle, ownership, evidence, escalation and gate model. The Audio Descriptions repository supplies AD-specific route selection, editorial practice, production steps, evaluation and delivery checks.

## Interpretation and boundaries

1. **Determine the access route for each actual version.** Options include sufficient existing main audio, audio-first or integrated description, separate AD, a hybrid, an extended described version, or descriptive live commentary. A no-separate-AD decision requires a version-specific visual audit, listening evidence, independent review and named approval; genre or a transcript alone is not sufficient.
2. **Preserve each source’s authority and strength.** Trace production controls to the production-management framework and AD practice to the Audio Descriptions guidance. Record whether an item is an external obligation, repository requirement, recommendation, or destination-specific profile. Do not silently elevate recommendations into universal requirements.
3. **Keep the human decision with the authorised person.** An agent may analyse, draft, track and prepare a decision. It cannot provide human editorial approval, specialist review, audience evidence or release authority.
4. **Evaluate the audience experience, not just the asset.** A completed script or delivered audio file does not establish that people can find, hear and use the described version.
5. **Scope decisions to exact versions and destinations.** A recut, changed language, mix, platform or delivery route may invalidate prior evidence and require reassessment.

